{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Downtrend bias: in falling regimes the model is consistently too high (positive bias), with the error growing with horizon. Apply a downward level shift that increases with lead time (small in days 1–5, larger by days 15–30).",
    "Insufficient downside momentum: the model underestimates the speed of sell-offs and predicts rebounds too early. Add extra negative drift when the last 1–2 weeks show persistent lower lows/lower highs.",
    "Overshoot on rebound/mean reversion: after a drop, the model projects a recovery to the low/mid-70s that does not materialize. Damp mean-reversion strength (cap rebound slope) unless there is clear trend reversal evidence in recent returns.",
    "Horizon-specific correction: errors are modest early but widen notably after ~day 10–15. Use piecewise calibration (e.g., minimal adjustment for days 1–7, moderate for days 8–14, strongest for days 15–30).",
    "Volatility asymmetry: upside moves are overpredicted relative to realized outcomes during stress periods. Reduce forecast volatility (shrink deviations from the near-term level) when recent realized volatility is elevated and trend is negative."
  ]
}
